  "question": {
    "text": "A standard cell of <math><mn>1.08</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> is balanced by the potential difference&nbsp;across <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>90</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> of a&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> meter, long wire supplied by a cell of emf <math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> through a series resistor of resistance <math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi></math>. If the internal resistance of cell in the primary circuit is zero then the resistance per unit length (in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>) of potentiometer wire is"
  },
